#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068547 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "f12b3891-021d-5ae3-a767-f9d423408ce6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3567" :question/problem "Is the function $y=f(x),$ plotted below, continuous at $x=0?$\n\n![](images/e3/e395863ca66a7e87fa335c56e5b6aeb1ec206893e57b155bf64e5ab07be7545c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Because there is an asymptote at $x=0,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nTherefore, the function $y=f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068548 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/id #uuid "514240a0-8a38-564f-ab4c-d0bacb0c5f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3568" :question/problem "What are the points of discontinuity of the function $y=g(x),$ plotted below?\n\n![](images/6d/6d0d3c2cd072841546e66d70105436200e316e156901f8fe27dc150e96ff3da4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The points of discontinuity are at $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and $x=2.5.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these three points without taking our pencil off of the page."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068549 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "01bca086-0ed4-5626-a357-e0724685f144" :question/math-academy-id "e-3569" :question/problem "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can see that the left and right-sided limits are different:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false.  The graph of the function breaks at $x=2.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62083efc-7529-5e30-8cd5-10a2ac34f827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548541} {:answer/id #uuid "7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548590} {:answer/id #uuid "9833b741-ee8c-5d09-8c5c-f6b3dd066d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548654} {:answer/id #uuid "b33c3fcf-3dab-5d9d-9d7d-61353468e8e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548715} {:answer/id #uuid "c0dbda4f-53b6-5cae-a016-7b51afc0e654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548590} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-104016" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9c49ba7f220352a8933159a854d6e311baaaf2b6ec1e1f6fbdb78988653a157f.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. $x=-3$\n\nII. $x=0$\n\nIII. $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-3,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is also continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"II and III only\".\n\n![](images/f9/f9c5342a872b4fd0ad2783b00d4d170b05844dc057a462832bf0eed41f50ccf4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60efdf1c-df12-5856-aa4c-f4f6fc23d42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437348} {:answer/id #uuid "cea4be79-bc3f-5364-9f0f-d13cc3390e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cea4be79-bc3f-5364-9f0f-d13cc3390e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437349} :answer-field/id #uuid "67208bb7-2119-51cc-97f2-fc43b1d95b36" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef42aad5-fee8-5449-bbd4-f1ddf4d65e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437350} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3c2501-c170-5ad7-8f83-f02c8744f9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef42aad5-fee8-5449-bbd4-f1ddf4d65e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437350} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f1b2ad2-b8cf-5da8-ab38-a32e460f880a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438704} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e135984-0cd0-5f07-bdfa-2c1791ff56da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437352} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac001f4-ce03-575e-80fe-299ba4a5012e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ac001f4-ce03-575e-80fe-299ba4a5012e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437353} :answer-field/id #uuid "f18fe838-0660-5c6a-8fe2-0675d1705648" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc9393f2-809c-5769-b329-186102da51c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104051" :question/problem "![](images/65/65b9b32e8fac6a71141ce820855058367e9235129de7334db35a200905474cc0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$\n\n- **II.** ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists\n\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d495799e-2f9d-5146-a399-506d9d32ad84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c9ba1e63c665ad63597c822828fdf26ec0fefade083df5ea828f57a49d3aa149.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392604} {:answer/id #uuid "c796e42f-5eb7-5d3a-900e-6d5fb58916e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fc/fc1d428500558ed62fbf5f7e0c13604cbc759629cedb63a8f9c2f7fc0b412480.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392605} {:answer/id #uuid "742220de-47f9-5f30-8679-f64626ac489f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d9185fb471c9806d490133c6ea5a54ef69878f89ca0e0be66008ec3626bfe346.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392606} {:answer/id #uuid "11ce7ab1-ca48-5c69-b7f7-b7ff62f3ea3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/49e7b3c3cd4e6410b95a307f74c90079ec61c2d3540f86a357db88c77039ca44.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392607} {:answer/id #uuid "c37ded1b-1a1e-52ed-a977-31d60b87248f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bd/bd662a57e07bf3f0319a00a15dc5c520a172d59672eae24f7999a4535da0b9ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c796e42f-5eb7-5d3a-900e-6d5fb58916e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fc/fc1d428500558ed62fbf5f7e0c13604cbc759629cedb63a8f9c2f7fc0b412480.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392605} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac8479b0-0f39-5a85-82ad-a7d9ee04b2a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3d5f3e1-0f7f-56ba-9eea-f720be7a7122" :question/math-academy-id "q-48038" :question/problem "Select the function that has a discontinuity at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is shown below. Notice that the function contains a break exactly at $x=1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n![](images/3c/3c8a198c1ec835590a3d755b964929ef9ac9a30e4268b2fdc80dacdb394899d8.png)\n\nAll the other given graphs can be plotted without taking the pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1338885-9fa5-56a6-bbde-464a7beff78d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439807} {:answer/id #uuid "c73173bd-c83c-504e-9f9a-d56025e0fdde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c73173bd-c83c-504e-9f9a-d56025e0fdde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439808} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb748d5b-bb9b-5e8c-884e-a291b4ee024f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440261} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9689ec4f-c734-5a92-8f3f-3bc8bc7f5697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439809} {:answer/id #uuid "e4abe095-a9a5-582e-9ac5-95107ec344b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9689ec4f-c734-5a92-8f3f-3bc8bc7f5697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439809} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8e06b7f-98e6-5fb5-9ff3-c9f6a2f96bc6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440262} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fe5f199-131e-595e-9f80-3a37e7df7a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439811} {:answer/id #uuid "da339f68-232e-501a-ad3b-79e19df38957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fe5f199-131e-595e-9f80-3a37e7df7a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439811} :answer-field/id #uuid "77f7cc9d-2122-5ca5-9609-e88412505ba8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440263}] :question/id #uuid "a302c934-1e7e-57a4-882d-8cd125952f61" :question/math-academy-id "q-48064" :question/problem "![](images/02/0218a1e13a0504b20e802100f9e15877beeb2b565a49fe7cd01cf2a0cf0197ee.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is defined at $x = 2$\n- **II.** $lim_(x → 2)f(x)$ exists\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 2$" :question/worked-solution "The filled point at x=2 gives f(2)=1. The left and right limits are 1 and -2, so the two-sided limit does not exist and the function is not continuous there."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a492d7e-03e3-53b9-b29f-5d23248be42a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548496} {:answer/id #uuid "a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548679} {:answer/id #uuid "c3616842-989f-5138-afaa-77cd88a676d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548744} {:answer/id #uuid "f7cc25a6-f144-5a0e-a8e0-dd12016dae8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548850} {:answer/id #uuid "ff301354-641f-5c50-8775-32e42d04d7ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548679} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48089" :question/problem "![](images/17/17932f6186d98bb68ff231c07ae1e2b42dbe7c391ff442db6d4e8c9da92a9a82.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=2$, meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is  $x=2$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid